  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Ridge of high pressure and abnormally hot temperatures in\n   control through end of the week peaking Friday. \n\n-  Elevated convection spreading northward Friday afternoon into\n   the overnight bringing localized lightning and gusty outflow \n   winds.\n\n-  Showers and thunderstorms with heavy rain potential in north-\n   central Idaho and southwest Montana for Saturday.\n\n\nThe Northern Rockies will remain under high pressure today and on\nFriday. Daytime high temperatures will be about 10 degrees above\nnormal, reaching mid 90s for Montana valleys and triple digits for\nthe lower valleys of Idaho. Peak temperatures will occur on\nFriday, when Heat Advisories will be in effect for the valleys of \nNez Perce and Idaho Counties of Idaho. \n\nLow pressure off the coast of the northwestern U.S., visible on\nsatellite imagery, will make its way onshore by Friday\nnight/Saturday morning. With the low to our west and the ridge\nmoving east, the Northern Rockies will be under southwest flow\naloft. Moisture from the southwest will interact with a shortwave\ntrough embedded in the southwesterly flow on Friday night,\nresulting in chances for overnight convection possibly lasting\ninto Saturday morning. With a dry lower atmosphere hanging on from\nearlier on Friday, storms will be moisture-limited, especially for\nSanders, Mineral, and Lincoln counties of northwest Montana. Much\nmore moisture becomes available by Saturday afternoon, along with\nenhanced instability with daytime heating. The thunderstorm \nthreat will be region-wide, with about a 5% chance for storms to \nbecome severe (58 mph wind gusts and/or one inch hail). Other \nthreats will be frequent lightning and heavy rain. Storms \nproducing heavy rain will be more likely over north-central Idaho \n(including Lemhi County) and southwest Montana, where precipitable\nwater values exceed one inch. There will be about a 5% chance for\nexcessive rainfall causing isolated flash flooding for the \naforementioned areas. \n\nThe Pacific low will move north of the Idaho Panhandle and western\nMontana on Sunday. While large-scale precipitation goes mostly\nnorth of our region, a cold front will move through the Northern\nRockies on Sunday. The front will bring increased westerly winds\nand drier conditions.",
